{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And laughed (וַיִּצְחָק)</b> Onkelos translates (Onkelos on Genesis 17:17) 'and rejoiced' (וַחֲדִי), and so it is, for the word צחוק (laughter) is sometimes used for mockery and sometimes for joy, as in 'rejoicing in the world, His earth' (Proverbs 8:31), 'rejoicing before HaShem' (II Samuel 6:5). And in my opinion, the intent of this expression is that whenever a person sees a wondrous thing happening to him for good, he rejoices until his mouth is filled with laughter — and this is what Sarah said (Genesis 21:6), 'Elohim has made laughter for me; everyone who hears will laugh with me,' as in 'then our mouth will be filled with laughter and our tongue with glad song' (Psalms 126:2). And so Abraham did when this was told to him: he rejoiced and filled his mouth with laughter, and he said in his heart, 'This is fitting for laughter, for it is a very wondrous thing — shall one who is a hundred years old be born, and shall Sarah at ninety give birth — and would this not be for laughter and joy?' And Scripture abbreviates in the exclamatory rhetorical question, for it is attached to what he said: 'And he laughed.' Similarly, 'Have I even here seen' (Genesis 16:13) is attached to the beginning of the verse, for she said: 'Have I even here seen HaShem who appeared to me — that He saw my affliction, and I shall not call Him El Ro'i?' And similarly, 'Did I reveal Myself to your father's house' (I Samuel 2:27) is attached to 'Why do you trample My sacrifice and My meal-offering,' meaning: Did I choose you so that you would trample My sacrifice and My meal-offering — and why do you do so? Alternatively, 'Shall one who is a hundred years old be born' may be a question of wonder, not of impossibility, like 'Will you judge the city of blood?' (Ezekiel 22:2), whose meaning is: Would you be willing to judge it and declare all its abominations? And similarly, 'Will you let your eyes fly after it, and it is gone?' (Proverbs 23:5). And similarly, 'From the tree which I commanded you not to eat — have you eaten?' (Genesis 3:11), meaning: Did it enter your mind and heart to eat from the tree? And so here: Would it enter the heart that one a hundred years old should be born, that Sarah at ninety years old should give birth? And afterward He told him that this miracle would come together with the life of Ishmael." "<b>Shall one who is a hundred years old be born (הַלְּבֶן מֵאָה שָׁנָה יִוָּלֵד)</b> There is no wonder in a hundred-year-old man begetting, for men beget all the days that they have vitality, even at ninety and a hundred, in these generations too — and all the more so in the days of Abraham, whose full years had not yet passed by twice over. And behold, forty years after this he begot many children from Keturah. Rather, 'shall one who is a hundred years old be born' means: he who did not beget in his youth from this woman shall now beget from her after a hundred years, and after ninety, since the way of women had ceased from her — for he knew this to be so. And therefore he did not say so at the beginning, when it was told to him (Genesis 17:6), 'I will make you nations,' until He said to him that Sarah would bear. And He commanded him, 'You shall call his name Yitzchak,' on account of the laughter that Abraham expressed — and this is a sign, for it was in faith and in joy. And after Abraham called him thus, as HaShem had commanded him, Sarah said: 'Did he call his name Yitzchak, because Elohim has made laughter for me?'"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The text of Abraham's inner monologue appears to continue directly into the rhetorical question from the verse; rendered hyper-literally to preserve the syntactic flow."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:28.057726527Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}